#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b901242c-eea0-5b94-9082-042ff7341911" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Over a Type I Region Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e571038-630a-58a2-b12e-f4dc681fc3aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344259} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bdb6b5-885b-5caf-8ae4-c62710a33fa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344260} {:answer/id #uuid "03aa948c-24e2-5c73-af2e-3bf28eb4e300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344261} {:answer/id #uuid "62c26b3b-4f49-5883-be15-ed9b7042def0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344262} {:answer/id #uuid "0262a338-3ef9-522b-afdb-3ee815dfa0a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8bdb6b5-885b-5caf-8ae4-c62710a33fa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344260} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9b1f369-2d23-5c18-a35e-df7f9bf8c793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd583b91-12f4-580a-8837-094e665d3931" :question/math-academy-id "q-161516" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/assets/9d6a1a5ca848d7563763a3384855daf13031c808852efa541a73e2ef3126c6ed.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}4xy\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above. \n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/assets/35113579a0c74805710c8787ba8738cd76c2777d59cf2a0cd234b284760ca6f6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-x+y},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/assets/012a2a575147df3cf7fc0a5b85443d461c77c8447797969d8b96d9007cbfffd2.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-x+y}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4xy\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x+y}4xy\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x+y}4xy\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-x+y}4xy\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[4xyz]_{z=0}^{z=3-x+y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4xy(3-x+y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12xy-4{x}^{2}y+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12xy-4{x}^{2}y+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12xy-4{x}^{2}y+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[6x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}x)-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{22}{3}x-2{x}^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{22}{3}x-2{x}^{2})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{11}{3}{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{11}{3}-\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
